Ah, here's a problem. Extropy isn't the goal, it's the means to the end.
Most of us want to promote extropy in order to make the universe a better
place to live in.

>Another take on this: Mark Miller's definition of immortality as
>"recognizing and being grateful to your predecessors."

That's a pretty hollow form of immortality, in my opinion. It certainly
has a ring of truth to it, but if I were Plato, I'd rather be able to
stand next to you and argue my point, continue to contribute and ENJOY
LIFE.

>Why not consider myself a rare species, worthy of preservation?  That's
>an interesting point, indeed, though it doesn't have the force of the
>discounted-in-my-worldview "my survival is the only important thing"
>cryonicist meme.

First of all, I doubt that many cryonicists would accept that meme. "My
survival is the most important thing," perhaps. There are still other
concerns.

Personally, I can't understand were you're coming from with this
ecologically collectivist argument. Why do you have to prove to the
"noosphere" your right to survive?

To whom do you have to prove your worthiness? Yourself!

Unfortunately, you seem to have to have already judged yourself not
worthy of preservation. Sad, really. It's not very sound evolutionary
strategy either. The self-preservation instinct *is* a survival trait,
you know.

