MP:I don't see how the "therefore" matches up with the "high".  If the pop-
MP:II stars have been burning for ages, wouldn't we expect them to be
MP:"slow-burners", and therefore rather dim.  Or is it that they are now
MP:rich in heavier fusion products, because they are so old, which is
MP:catalysing further fusion?

Mike:

I think Steven's "Note" about extreme Population II stars was
confusing also.  Extreme Population II stars are exactly that-
"extreme," and bringing them into a discussion of main sequence stars
was probably not very helpful.  Here's some added info about stellar
evolution classifications that may shed some light(!).

It is conventional in astronomy to divide stars into a relatively
young class with blue giants as the most luminous members, called
Population I, and an old class with red giants as the most luminous
members, called Population II.  But astronomers know that there exists
a range between these two, and it kind of looks like this: (1)

Population class	Typical Members
-------------------------------------------------------
Young Pop I		Blue giants, galactic clusters
Intermediate Pop I	Strong-line stars, solar neighborhood
Old Pop I		Weak-line stars
Mild Pop II		Majority of high-velocity stars
Extreme Pop II		Bright red giants, globular clusters, 
			subdwarfs

Population II stars:
The material from which our Galaxy was made was probably mostly
hydrogen, with a small amount of helium, and very tiny amounts of
other elements. In its early stages, when the first stars formed, the
Galaxy had not yet collapsed to the flattened disk-like shape we see
now. The first stars were formed out of nearly pure hydrogen, and had
a more or less spherical distribution about the Galaxy. These early
stars are believed to have formed in globular clusters, which have
retained their spherical distribution about the center of the
galaxy. Thus the oldest objects we see today are spherically
distributed about the Galactic center, and are very poor in heavy
elements- i.e. they are nearly pure hydrogen.  (2)

Population I stars:
By contrast, the youngest stars we see in our Galaxy must have been
recently formed out of material within the Galactic disk, since that
is where most of the potential protostar material lies. Furthermore,
the process of stellar evolution causes a slow enrichment of the
interstellar material from which stars are formed, hence the youngest
stars we see must be relatively rich in heavy elements. (2)

Why are Population II stars frequently more luminous?

It is as Steven Webb said, because they have more hydrogen, and here
is the reason. Because of the great binding energy of the helium
nucleus, the most abundant source of energy in stellar interiors is
the fusion of four hydrogen atoms into one helium atom. The energy
given off by the conversion of hydrogen to helium comes very close to
being the maximum amount of energy that can be liberated by nuclear
reactions in stellar interiors. The conversion of hydrogen to helium
releases 7/8 of the total energy available from nuclear reactions.

About the Sun.

Because of all of the above, we would expect that stars formed
relatively recently would be richest in metals and would therefore
show the strongest metallic (spectroscopic) lines. Population I stars
formed in the more distant past are expected to be weak-line stars. By
careful spectroscopic comparison with other G stars, the Sun has been
classified as a weak-line 4.6 billion-year-old star, i.e. a relatively
old Population I star. (An age of 5 billion years is thought of as
representative of the oldest of what astronomers call Population I
stars.)

About the age of the Universe.

Have you been following the recent reports of the Hubble Observations
that have resulted in newer calculations of the Hubble constant?
These newer Hubble constant calculations show the age of the Universe
to be about 8 billion years old. Astronomers used to place the oldest
stars (Population II) as 10-20 billion years old. How can the age of
the Universe be younger than that of the oldest stars? The astronomers
are in a tizzy about this, as you can imagine.

So I hope this little lesson in stellar evolution clarifies things...

Amara


(1) Clayton, Donald, _Principles of Stellar Evolution and
Nucleosynthesis_, 1983, Chicago, IL: U of Chicago Press, pp 61.

(2) Bowers, Richard and Deeming, Terry, _Astrophysics I: Stars_,
Boston, MA: Jones and Bartlett Publishers, 1984, pp 35.


